  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>{</mo><msub><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>&#160;</mi><msub><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#8230;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><msub><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></msub><mo>}</mo></math> and <math><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#160;</mi><mo>{</mo><msub><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>&#160;</mi><msub><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>&#160;</mi><msub><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>}</mo></math><br /><br />Let us select <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math> elements from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> &amp; connect it to <math><msub><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2&#160;</mn></mrow></msub></math></p><p>Number of ways&nbsp;<math><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mi>&#160;</mi></math><br /><br />The number of ways would be equal to&nbsp;<math><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mi>&#160;</mi></math> and now we have 2 elements <math><msub><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>&#160;</mi><mo>&#38;</mo><mi>&#160;</mi><msub><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mi>&#160;</mi></math> in set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> to be mapped from the remaining element of set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math></p><p>Hence, total number of function&nbsp;<math><msup><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></math>&nbsp;and out of which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> would be &quot;into&quot; functions (when all four goes in <math><msub><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math> &amp; when all four goes in <math><msub><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></math> <math><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn></math><br /><br /><math><mo>&#8756;</mo></math>So the total number of onto functions would be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>14</mn><msup><mo>&#183;</mo><mn>7</mn></msup><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>",
